780:
(a) Yard trimmings shall be sorted and stored in the following manner:
(1) Yard trimmings shall be placed at curbside on the day of collection, separate and apart from other municipal solid waste. All yard trimmings shall be placed as near as possible to the street or sidewalk right-of-way adjacent to the property owner's property, but not so as to get into the street or block the sidewalk. At no time shall yard trimmings be placed in gutters, drains, walkways, alleys or streets of the city.
(2) All persons engaged in the business of building, clearing of land, clearing of trees, roots and stumps, removal of refuse and debris from premises on which the refuse was or is located, shall have the full responsibility to dispose of such debris, refuse, stumps, trees or other waste by hauling it to a private disposal facility. Except as otherwise provided in this subsection, no such waste shall be piled or placed upon adjoining premises or upon any property which is not permitted by the state environmental protection division as a private waste dump within the city.
(3) To assist the city in reducing the amount of yard trimmings to be disposed of, the city adopts and recommends the following hierarchy to property owners for handling yard trimmings:
a. Implementation of naturalized, low-maintenance landscaping requiring little or no cutting;
b. Implementation of grass cycling by mowing it high and letting it lie;
c. The stacking of branches into brush piles for use as wildlife habitats and for gradual decomposition into the soil;
d. Composting on the site where the material was grown, followed by incorporation of the finished compost into the soil at that site;
e. Chipping woody material on the site where such material was generated;
f. Chipping woody material for later use as fiber fuel; and
g. If subsection (a)(3)a.—f. of this section is implemented and still results in yard trimmings which need to be transported to another site, the requirements of section 70-107 and subsections (a)(1) and (a)(2) of this section should be complied with.
(4) Every person providing contractual services for residential or business property shall be responsible for the disposal of all waste generated as a result of the services rendered. Examples include, but are not limited to, tree cutting and trimming, shrub or vegetation cutting and trimming, remodeling, renovations, landscaping or any other services provided or performed on said premises. The city will not be responsible for the collection and removable of any such waste generated on said property by said services performed.
(b) Any person violating subsection (a) of this section may be punished by a fine not to exceed $1,000.00 or imprisonment of 12 months, or both, as is provided for in the Fort Oglethorpe City Charter, section 4.13.
(Ord. No. 97-16, § 1(c), (d), 11-14-1997; Ord. No. 2005-01, 3-14-2005)

801:
Any resident who wants to have the name of a road changed must submit a petition to the city manager for verification. The city manager and the Fort Oglethorpe Fire Chief will make recommendations to the city council after the following criteria have been met:
(1) A nonrefundable fee as stated in the schedule of fees and charges for each road shall accompany any petition requesting that an existing road name be changed;
(2) The petition shall include the existing road name, the proposed road name, and the signature of the persons residing adjacent to the road who want the name changed;
(3) The city manager shall cause the request to be advertised pursuant to the applicable city ordinances; and
(4) If the city manager and the city council approve the request, the petitioners shall be required to pay the city for the cost of purchasing and erecting new road signs.
(Ord. No. 97-76, § 9, 10-27-1997; Ord. No. 2002-04, § 1, 4-22-2002)

824:
The regulations of this chapter are adopted for the following purposes:
(1) Protect and provide for the public health, safety, morals and general welfare of the city;
(2) Provide for adequate light, air and privacy; to secure safety from fire, flood and other danger; and to prevent overcrowding of the land and undue congestion of population;
(3) Protect the character and the social and economic stability of all parts of the city and to encourage the orderly and beneficial development of all parts of the city;
(4) Protect and conserve the value of land throughout the city and the value of buildings and improvements upon the land and to minimize the conflicts among the uses of land and buildings;
(5) Guide public and private policy and action in order to provide adequate and efficient transportation, water, sewerage, schools, recreation, and other public requirements and facilities;
(6) Preserve the natural beauty and topography of the city and to ensure appropriate development with regard to these natural features; and
(7) Ensure the wise development of the city in harmony with the comprehensive plan of the city.
(Ord. No. 95-21, § 1.00(A), 11-13-1995)

